{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 36, "model_name": "stockmark/stockmark-2-100b-instruct", "content": "the social network is an engrossing tale of the creation and meteoric rise of facebook, as told through the eyes of its founder mark zuckerberg. the film opens with him being sued by his former best friend and co-founder edward saverin, setting up a narrative that intertwines legal disputes with flashbacks of zuckerberg's college days. the story delves into his motivations, the ruthless ambition of a young man who saw an opportunity to connect people globally, and the personal and professional conflicts that arose along the way.\n\nperformance-wise, jesse Eisenberg's portrayal of mark zuckerberg is nothing short of brilliant. he captures the complex psyche of a genius who is simultaneously awkward, arrogant, and deeply insecure. Eisenberg's nuanced performance makes zuckerberg a sympathetic yet flawed character, one that the audience can't help but be both intrigued and slightly repelled by. the supporting cast, including andrew garfield as saverin, is equally strong, each actor bringing depth to their roles and contributing to the film's overall tension.\n\ndirector david fincher crafts a visually stunning film, with a sleek, fast-paced editing style that mirrors the digital age it portrays. the score by trent reznor and atticus ross is another highlight, its pulsating, electronic sounds adding to the film's dynamic energy. the visuals, from the dimly lit dorm rooms to the sleek silicon valley offices, effectively contrast zuckerberg's personal journey with the rapid expansion of his company.\n\nthe social network is not just a biopic, but a commentary on the modern era's obsession with social media, the ethical ambiguities of entrepreneurship, and the price of success. while it doesn't shy away from zuckerberg's flaws, it also acknowledges the transformative power of his creation. the film leaves the audience with a lot to ponder about the 